1. Yes, a number of them were. Since April 1, 1980, bilingualism bonus recipients have had to be confirmed annually, at the time of their official employee performance appraisal, as still meeting the language requirements of their position in order to continue receiving bonus payments. The Language Knowledge Examination (LKE) can be used for this purpose under the following circumstances:
(a)	when there is no linguistically qualified supervisor to confirm the employee's eligibility;
(b)	if the employee is not exempted from further testing by the Public Service Commission as a result of demonstrated high proficiency in the second official language;
(c)	if the employee does not possess LKE results which fall within the one and two year periods of validity established by the Public Service Commission; and
(d)	if an employee does not agree with the supervisor's assessment, he or she may have recourse to an LKE
2. See 1 above.
